TAR(1)                         P32W Unix                          TAR(1)

NAME
       tar - tape archiver, POSIX ustar format

SYNOPSIS
       tar c[vzf] [archive] file ...
       tar t[vzf] [archive] [name ...]
       tar x[vzf] [archive] [name ...]

DESCRIPTION
       tar packs files into one archive file and unpacks them again.
       The archives are POSIX ustar, so they are exchangeable with
       the Mac's tar (bsdtar) and GNU tar.

       The first argument is a word of key letters; a leading - is
       allowed (tar -xvf). Exactly one of c, t and x says what to do:

       c      Create a new archive holding the files. A directory is
              archived with everything below it.

       t      List the archive: the names, one per line.

       x      Extract the archive into the current directory.

       With t and x, names select what to list or extract: an entry
       is taken if its name is one of the names or lies below one of
       them (name/...). Without names, every entry is taken. Names
       must be given as they are stored in the archive; there is no
       pattern matching. A name that matches no entry is reported
       ("not found in the archive") at the end.

KEYS
       f      The next argument is the archive. If it is -, or if
              there is no f, the archive is the standard output (c)
              or the standard input (t, x).

       v      Verbose. With c and x, each name is written to the
              standard error as it is done, after a or x. With t, a
              long listing, a line per entry:

              -rw-r--r-- ken/staff      123 2026-10-14 09:30 notes

              the type (- d c b p l, h for a hard link) and the
              permission bits as ls(1) shows them, the owner and
              group (their names in the archive, else the numbers),
              the size, the modification date and time (local time,
              by TZ) and the name.

       z      Pass the archive through gzip(1): /bin/gzip -c when
              creating, /bin/gzip -dc when listing or extracting.

       Keys may come in any order; if several of c, t, x are given,
       the last one counts.

       WHAT IS ARCHIVED
       Regular files, directories, character and block special files
       (with their major and minor numbers) and FIFOs. A file with
       several links is stored once; its other names in the archive
       become hard-link entries. Each entry keeps its mode (all 12
       bits), user and group ids and names, and modification time.
       Names are stored as given (a directory with a trailing /),
       less a leading /: tar says "removing leading / from member
       names" once and stores /usr/ken/x as usr/ken/x, so the
       archive extracts below the current directory. The archive
       itself, if it is met among the files (tar cf a.tar .), is
       left out with a message. A
       name longer than 100 characters is split at a / into the
       ustar prefix (up to 155) and name fields; if that is
       impossible tar says "name too long" and leaves the file out.
       The archive is written in records of 10240 bytes (20 blocks
       of 512) and ends with two zero blocks, padded to a whole
       record.

       WHAT IS EXTRACTED
       Directories that a name needs are made as necessary. An
       existing file is removed first, then the entry is written
       anew; files are first created mode 0600, and get their own
       mode and modification time once they are complete.
       Directories get theirs after the whole archive is read (so a
       read-only directory can still be filled). When run by root,
       tar also sets the owners: by user and group name if this
       system knows the name, else by the number in the archive.
       Others become the owner of what they extract.

       Hard links are made with link(2), so the file linked to must
       be extracted first (as it is when the whole archive is
       extracted). Special files and FIFOs are made with mknod(2),
       which needs root. Entries with an absolute name or a .. in
       the name are not extracted.

       Also read: the pax extended headers that macOS tar writes
       (their path and linkpath are used, the rest is ignored),
       pax global headers (ignored), GNU long names and long link
       names, and old (pre-POSIX) tar headers. Symbolic links are
       reported and skipped, since P32W Unix has none; other unknown
       entry types are reported and skipped. Either makes the exit
       status 1.

       Times in the archive are UTC, as the format requires.

EXAMPLES
       tar cvf /mnt/ken.tar /usr/ken     archive a home directory
       tar czf src.tgz src               a gzip-compressed archive
       tar tvf src.tar                   list it, long form
       tar xf src.tar src/cmd            extract one subtree
       tar xzf src.tgz                   extract a compressed one
       tar cf - doc | gzip > doc.tar.gz  the same with a pipe

       On the Mac, leave out the ._name AppleDouble files that macOS
       tar adds by making the archive with

              COPYFILE_DISABLE=1 tar cf files.tar files

EXIT STATUS
       0 if all went well; 1 if a file could not be read, archived
       or extracted, an entry was skipped (a symbolic link, an
       unknown type, an absolute name or ..), or a name was not
       found in the archive (tar says which and goes on); 2 for a usage
       error, an archive that cannot be opened, read or written, a
       header checksum error (not a tar archive), a truncated
       archive, or a failure of gzip with z.

SEE ALSO
       gzip(1), zip(1), unzip(1), kermit(1), ar(1), ln(1), chown(1)

BUGS
       Only c, t, x, v, f and z: no r, u (append, update), no C
       (change directory), no m, o, p, no exclusion and no
       wildcards in names.

       Entries with an absolute name (made by another tar) are
       refused by x, not extracted with the / removed.

       File sizes and times are stored in 32 bits (files up to 4 GiB,
       which P32W cannot hold anyway); device major and minor numbers
       in 8 bits each. Path names are limited to 255 characters,
       pax and GNU long names included.

       With x, an entry that names an existing directory as a plain
       file fails; tar does not remove directories.
